The '''1999 FA Cup final''' was an [[association football]] match that took place on 22 May 1999 at the old [[Wembley Stadium (1923)|Wembley Stadium]] in [[London]] to determine the winner of the [[1998–99 FA Cup]]. It was contested between [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] and [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]]. Goals from [[Teddy Sheringham]] and [[Paul Scholes]] gave Manchester United a 2–0 win to claim their 10th [[FA Cup]] title. It was the second part of the [[Treble (association football)|treble]] of trophies Manchester United won during the 1998–99 season as they won the [[1998-99 FA Premier League|Premier League]] the previous weekend and had the [[1999 UEFA Champions League Final|Champions League final]] four days later, which they ended up winning.

Manchester United faced [[Premier League]] opposition in every round of the competition except the fifth. Their [[FA Cup semi-finals|semi-final]] against the cup holders, [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]], was the last in the history of the competition to go to a replay; after a 0–0 draw in the original match, Manchester United won the replay 2–1. In the other semi-final, Newcastle beat [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] 2–0.

As Manchester United had already qualified for the [[1999–2000 UEFA Champions League]], England's place in the [[1999–2000 UEFA Cup]] was given to Newcastle United as the runners-up. Manchester United did not defend their FA Cup title, choosing instead to participate in the [[2000 FIFA Club World Championship]] in Brazil, believing that it would help [[The Football Association]]'s bid to host the [[2006 FIFA World Cup]] (which was eventually awarded to the [[German Football Association]]). As winners of the FA Cup, Manchester United also qualified for the [[1999 FA Charity Shield]] but as they already earned their place as Premier League champions, the cup berth was given to league runners-up Arsenal.

==Background==
[[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] went into the match as [[List of English football champions|champions of England]], having clinched the [[Premier League]] title in their final game the previous weekend after losing just three league games all season.<ref>{{cite news |title=Glorious United crowned champions |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/fa_carling_premiership/345445.stm |website=BBC News |date=27 May 1999 |access-date=23 August 2015 }}</ref><ref name="leaguetable">{{cite web |title=League Table – 1998/99 |url=http://www.premierleague.com/en-gb/matchday/league-table.html?season=1998-1999&month=MAY&timelineView=date&toDate=1116111600000&tableView=CURRENT_STANDINGS |website=premierleague.com |publisher=Premier League |access-date=23 August 2015 |archive-date=26 September 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150926113824/http://www.premierleague.com/en-gb/matchday/league-table.html?season=1998-1999&month=MAY&timelineView=date&toDate=1116111600000&tableView=CURRENT_STANDINGS |url-status=dead }}</ref> They had also qualified for the [[1999 UEFA Champions League Final]] against [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]], due to be played four days later on 26 May, and were unbeaten in their previous 31 matches in all competitions.<ref name="two_teams">{{cite news |title=Two teams, one trophy |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/fa_cup/349401.stm |website=BBC News |date=22 May 1999 |access-date=24 March 2014 }}</ref> By contrast, [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] had failed to win any of their final seven league matches; since their last win (against [[Derby County F.C.|Derby County]] on 3 April), their only victory had come in the FA Cup semi-final against [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Michael |last=Walker |title=Gullit left appealing to Newcastle's subconscious |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/1999/may/17/match.sport5 |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News & Media |date=17 May 1999 |access-date=12 June 2020 }}</ref> Newcastle finished 13th out of the 20 teams in the Premier League,<ref name="leaguetable"/> and were knocked out of the [[1998–99 UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] in the first round and the [[1998–99 Football League Cup|League Cup]] in the fourth round.<ref>{{cite web |title=Newcastle United FC – History |url=http://www.uefa.com/teamsandplayers/teams/club=59324/profile/history/index.html |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=23 August 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170727001652/http://www.uefa.com/teamsandplayers/teams/club=59324/profile/history/index.html |archive-date=27 July 2017 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=1998-99 Football League Cup |url=http://fchd.info/cups/leaguecup1998-99.htm |website=FCHD.info |publisher=Football Club History Database |access-date=23 August 2015 }}</ref>

It was Newcastle's second consecutive appearance in the [[FA Cup Final]] – having lost 2–0 to [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] in [[1998 FA Cup final|1998]] – and their 13th appearance overall.<ref name="facupfinals">{{cite web |title=FA Cup Finals, 1872–today |url=http://www.thefa.com/thefacup/more/finals |website=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |access-date=23 August 2015 }}</ref> They had a 50&nbsp;percent record in their previous 12 finals.<ref name="facupfinals"/> Manchester United had a better record in their 14 previous final appearances, having won on a record nine occasions; the last two, in [[1994 FA Cup final|1994]] and [[1996 FA Cup final|1996]], had been part of [[Double (association football)|doubles]].<ref name="facupfinals"/> With victory in the 1999 final, Manchester United would become the first English club to win the Double on three occasions, and it would put them one win away from a [[Treble (association football)|continental treble]] of the league, the FA Cup and the [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]], a feat no English team had ever managed before.<ref name="two_teams"/>

The two teams had previously met 128 times in all competitions; Manchester United had won 60 times, Newcastle United 37 times, and the remaining 31 matches had finished as draws.<ref name="record">{{cite web |title=United versus Newcastle United |url=http://www.stretfordend.co.uk/opponents/newcastle.html |website=StretfordEnd.co.uk |access-date=23 August 2013 }}</ref> Only two of those meetings were in the FA Cup: the first took place in the [[1908–09 FA Cup|1908–09 semi-final]], when Manchester United won 1–0 at [[Bramall Lane]] in [[Sheffield]] on the way to their first FA Cup title, and the second in the fifth round of the [[1989–90 FA Cup|1989–90 competition]], when Manchester United won 3–2 at [[St James' Park]] before going on to win their seventh FA Cup.<ref name="record"/> Manchester United also came out on top in their two league meetings in the 1998–99 season, winning 2–1 at St James' Park on 13 March after playing out a goalless draw at Old Trafford on 8 November.<ref name="record"/>

This was the first FA Cup Final that would not be replayed in the event of a draw. Instead, the match would be decided on the day, first with a period of [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]] then a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] if the scores were still level.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=FA Cup in danger of losing lustre |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/4235997.stm |website=BBC Sport |date=4 February 2005 |access-date=12 June 2020 }}</ref>

