As Premier League clubs, both Manchester United and Newcastle United entered the [[1998–99 FA Cup]] in the third round proper. Newcastle were drawn at home to [[Football League First Division|First Division]] side [[Crystal Palace F.C.|Crystal Palace]], and Manchester United at home to fellow Premier League side [[Middlesbrough F.C.|Middlesbrough]], whose 3–2 win at Old Trafford in the league two weeks earlier would turn out to be Manchester United's last defeat all season.<ref>{{cite web |title=1998/99 |url=http://www.stretfordend.co.uk/seasons/season1999.html |website=StretfordEnd.co.uk |access-date=17 June 2020 }}</ref> Despite the team's [[Goalkeeper (association football)|goalkeeper]], [[Shay Given]], being sent off within the first 15 minutes and then going 1–0 down, Newcastle came from behind to beat Crystal Palace in their tie,<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup highlights |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/246647.stm |website=BBC News |date=3 January 1999 |access-date=25 March 2014 }}</ref> while Manchester United also overturned Middlesbrough's 1–0 lead to win 3–1.<ref>{{cite news |title=United set up clash of the giants |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/247413.stm |website=BBC News |date=3 January 1999 |access-date=25 March 2014 }}</ref>

Manchester United's victory set up a fourth round tie at home to [[Liverpool F.C.–Manchester United F.C. rivalry|arch-rivals]] [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]], and Newcastle were paired with First Division [[Bradford City A.F.C.|Bradford City]].<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup 4th round draw |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/247492.stm |website=BBC News |date=13 January 1999 |access-date=25 March 2014 }}</ref> Newcastle won 3–0 to book their place in the fifth round,<ref>{{cite news |title=Brave Bradford overcome by Newcastle |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/261332.stm |website=BBC News |date=25 January 1999 |access-date=25 March 2014 }}</ref> while Manchester United again came from a goal down to beat Liverpool with two goals in the last two minutes of their tie.<ref>{{cite news |title=United pull off Cup smash-and-grab |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/261973.stm |website=BBC News |date=24 January 1999 |access-date=25 March 2014 }}</ref>

The fifth round saw Newcastle drawn at home to their first Premier League opposition of the tournament in [[Blackburn Rovers F.C.|Blackburn Rovers]], whereas Manchester United were paired with their only non-Premier League opponents, [[Football League Second Division|Second Division]] side [[Fulham F.C.|Fulham]]. A goal from [[Andy Cole]] saw Manchester United win 1–0 to progress to the sixth round,<ref>{{cite news |title=Cole leaves Fulham cold |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/fa_cup/279342.stm |website=BBC News |date=14 February 1999 |access-date=26 March 2014 }}</ref> but Newcastle were held to a goalless draw by Blackburn, forcing a replay. Newcastle [[Forward (association football)|striker]] [[Louis Saha]], on [[Loan (sports)|loan]] from [[FC Metz|Metz]], scored the only goal of the replay, and they were through to the last eight.<ref>{{cite news |title=Saha makes it simple for Ruud |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/fa_cup/285482.stm |website=BBC News |date=24 February 1999 |access-date=26 March 2014 }}</ref>

Home draws in the sixth round for both teams ensured that they had both been drawn at home in every round of the competition; Manchester United hosted [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] at [[Old Trafford]] and Newcastle hosted [[Everton F.C.|Everton]] at St James' Park. This time it was Newcastle who only needed one match to progress to the [[FA Cup semi-finals|semi-finals]], beating Everton 4–1 with goals from [[Temur Ketsbaia]] (2), [[Georgios Georgiadis (footballer, born 1972)|Georgios Georgiadis]] and [[Alan Shearer]].<ref>{{cite news |title=George and Georgian are Geordie heroes |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/fa_cup/292201.stm |website=BBC News |date=7 March 1999 |access-date=26 March 2014 }}</ref> Meanwhile, Manchester United were unable to make their numerical advantage count against Chelsea after a bad tackle on [[Paul Scholes]] earned [[Roberto Di Matteo]] a second yellow card, Scholes himself was sent off for Manchester United.<ref>{{cite news |title=Two off in FA Cup stalemate |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/fa_cup/292213.stm |website=BBC News |date=7 March 1999 |access-date=26 March 2014 }}</ref> The match finished goalless and a replay followed three days later; [[Dwight Yorke]] scored in each half to give Manchester United a 2–0 win.<ref>{{cite news |title=Deadly Dwight beats the Blues |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/fa_cup/294465.stm |website=BBC News |date=10 March 1999 |access-date=26 March 2014 }}</ref>

As per tradition,<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup: Greg Dyke says semi-finals will stay at Wembley |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27008308 |work=BBC Sport |date=13 April 2014 |access-date=6 May 2021 }}</ref> the semi-finals were played at neutral venues; Manchester United took on [[Arsenal F.C.–Manchester United F.C. rivalry|rivals]] Arsenal at [[Villa Park]] in [[Birmingham]], and Newcastle United played against Tottenham Hotspur at Old Trafford. Both semi-finals went to extra-time, but only Newcastle were able to produce a result on the day; after referee [[Paul Durkin]] missed a handball by [[Nikos Dabizas]] that would have resulted in a Tottenham penalty in normal time, Shearer scored twice in the second half of extra time to send Newcastle to [[Wembley Stadium (1923)|Wembley]] for the second year in a row.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shearer puts Newcastle in final |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/fa_cup/316805.stm |website=BBC News |date=11 April 1999 |access-date=26 March 2014 }}</ref> Manchester United's semi-final ended 0–0, although a goal from [[Roy Keane]] was ruled out for offside against Yorke, who was stood in the centre of the pitch not interfering with play, after [[Ryan Giggs]] had played the ball to himself in the build-up. After watching a replay of the incident, Manchester United manager [[Alex Ferguson]] claimed the decision was "absolutely ridiculous".<ref>{{cite news |first=Steve |last=Millar |title=Fergie Fury At Offside Howler |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/Football%3A+Fergie+Fury+At+Offside+Howler%3B+Man+Utd+0+Arsenal+0.-a060389139 |newspaper=The Mirror |publisher=Trinity Mirror |via=The Free Library |date=12 April 1999 |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref>

The replay (the last semi-final replay in the history of the FA Cup) is often included in lists of the greatest matches in the history of the tournament.<ref>{{cite news|title=FA Cup classic matches |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/30642478 |access-date=9 June 2020 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Paul |last=Higham |title=The best ever FA Cup semi-finals: We run down the Top 10 FA Cup semi-finals with classics through the years |url=https://www.sportinglife.com/football/news/top-10-fa-cup-semi-finals/179245 |website=sportinglife |publisher=Hestview |date=19 April 2020 |access-date=9 June 2020 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Sam |last=Cox |title=Top 5 FA Cup Matches Of All-Time |url=https://www.888sport.com/blog/football/top-5-fa-cup-matches-all-time-fa-cup-betting |work=888sport.com |publisher=The 888 Group |date=26 February 2020 |access-date=9 June 2020 }}</ref> [[David Beckham]] opened the scoring with a curving shot past Arsenal goalkeeper [[David Seaman]] from 22 yards, but [[Dennis Bergkamp]] equalised halfway through the second half, his shot deflecting off the knee of [[Jaap Stam]]. [[Nicolas Anelka]] had the ball in the back of the net for Arsenal again shortly afterwards, and their players had already begun celebrating before anyone realised that the linesman had raised his flag for an offside against Anelka. Roy Keane then received a second [[Penalty card|booking]] and was sent off for a cynical foul on [[Marc Overmars]], leaving Manchester United to play out the match with 10 men. The match was heading for extra time when [[Phil Neville]] made a "rash" challenge on [[Ray Parlour]] inside the penalty area; Bergkamp took the penalty, but [[Peter Schmeichel]] guessed the direction of his kick correctly and made the save. The first half of extra time passed without incident, before [[Patrick Vieira]] gave the ball away to Giggs at the start of the second; Giggs ran from just inside his own half, taking on four Arsenal [[Defender (association football)|defenders]] as he drove into the penalty area, then shot from a narrow angle over the head of Seaman into the roof of the Arsenal net. Manchester United hung on for the remaining 10 minutes to secure their place in the final.<ref>{{cite news |title=Giggs magic sinks Gunners |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/fa_cup/319696.stm |website=BBC News |date=14 April 1999 |access-date=26 March 2014 }}</ref>

==Pre-match==
===Team selection===
[[File:1999 FA Cup Final teams line up (cropped).jpg|thumb|The teams line up for the national anthem and presentations ahead of the final.]]
Manchester United decided to rest several star players for the match so that they could be fit for the Champions League final against Bayern Munich four days later. [[Midfielder]]s Roy Keane and Paul Scholes were suspended for that match,<ref>{{cite news |first=Sachin |last=Nakrani |title=Nine other players who have missed major finals due to two bookings |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2012/apr/25/chelsea-champions-league-suspensions |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News & Media |date=25 April 2012 |access-date=15 May 2021 }}</ref> so they were paired in central midfield; their deputy for the match against Bayern Munich, [[Nicky Butt]], was left out of the matchday squad entirely to guard him against injury. Top scorer Dwight Yorke was dropped to the bench for a similar reason, while first-choice [[Defender (association football)#Centre-back|centre-back]] Jaap Stam was named among the [[Substitute (association football)|substitutes]] to give him a chance to prove his fitness after an [[Achilles tendon]] injury. [[Denis Irwin]] missed the match due to suspension after being sent off against Liverpool in the Premier League on 5 May and was replaced in the starting line-up by Phil Neville.<ref name="two_teams"/><ref>{{cite news |title=Old-boy Ince robs United |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/fa_carling_premiership/336523.stm |website=BBC News |date=5 May 1999 |access-date=24 March 2014 }}</ref> Several players, including Scholes, David Beckham and [[Gary Neville]], were suffering from a flu virus in the lead-up to the game, and were still suffering the effects on matchday, but were all deemed fit enough to play.<ref name="delight">{{cite news |title=Delight for Fergie |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/fa_cup/350401.stm |website=BBC News |date=22 May 1999 |access-date=23 March 2014 }}</ref> [[Henning Berg]] was injured,<ref>{{cite news |first=Henry |last=Winter |title=Berg battles on |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2431205/Berg-battles-on.html |work=The Daily Telegraph |date=22 February 2002 |access-date=28 May 2019 }}</ref> so [[Ronny Johnsen]] and [[David May (footballer)|David May]] started in defence.<ref>{{cite news |first=Joe |last=Ganley |title=Treble Icons: Ronny Johnsen |url=https://www.manutd.com/en/news/detail/why-man-united-treble-icon-ronny-johnsen-was-a-lynchpin-behind-our-1999-success |website=ManUtd.com |publisher=Manchester United |date=25 April 2019 |access-date=9 June 2020 }}</ref>

For Newcastle United, the FA Cup final was their last game of the season, meaning that they were uninhibited in their squad selection for the match; however, manager [[Ruud Gullit]]'s final team was unpredictable and the players were unsure as to who would be picked until the day of the game. Goalkeeper [[Steve Harper]] recalled being unsure of whether it would be himself or Shay Given who would start in goal, midfielder [[Rob Lee]] said he had "resigned [himself] to not playing in the final", and right-back [[Warren Barton]] said he thought he was "in the team until the Wednesday";<ref name="gullit_shearer">{{cite news |first1=Chris |last1=Waugh |first2=George |last2=Caulkin |title=Gullit v Shearer: Wembley, white socks and a dressing room war |url=https://theathletic.co.uk/1827895/2020/05/22/gullit-v-shearer-wembley-white-socks-war-ruud-god-barton-lee-solano-harper/ |work=The Athletic |publisher=The Athletic Media Company |date=21 May 2020 |access-date=12 June 2020 }}</ref> Harper was ultimately selected in goal,<ref name="gullit_shearer"/> while the right-back position was given to 20-year-old [[Andy Griffin]], who had the job of marking Manchester United [[Midfielder#Winger|winger]] Ryan Giggs, having performed a similar role against Tottenham's [[David Ginola]] in the semi-final.<ref name="two_teams"/> According to the BBC, the biggest selection dilemma for Gullit was whom to pick to play up front alongside captain Alan Shearer: Georgian Ketsbaia or Scottish forward [[Duncan Ferguson]], who was due for an operation on a herniated groin two days later. Ketsbaia was selected to start, with Ferguson named as a substitute.<ref name="two_teams"/>

===Kits===
Manchester United wore their first-choice kit of red shirts, white shorts and black socks for the final.<ref>{{cite web |title=Season 1998/99 |url=http://www.unitedkits.com/kits/seasons/1998.php |website=unitedkits.com |access-date=12 June 2020 }}</ref> Had Newcastle worn their traditional first-choice kit, there would have been a clash between the two sides' socks; however, Gullit requested that his players wear white socks with black trim rather than their usual black socks with white trim, citing the success of his former teams who had also worn white socks.<ref name="gullit_shearer"/>

==Match==
===Summary===
====First half====
[[File:1999 FA Cup Final Solano free kick (cropped).jpg|thumb|left|Newcastle's [[Nolberto Solano]] prepares to take a free kick on the edge of the Manchester United box.]]
Newcastle United kicked off the match, attacking from east to west, and fashioned the first chance after three minutes; Shearer's header to [[Nolberto Solano]] allowed the Peruvian to volley on target, but the strike was not clean and Manchester United goalkeeper Schmeichel gathered it at the second attempt.<ref name="harris_296">Harris (2019), p. 296.</ref> Meanwhile, tackles from [[Dietmar Hamann]] and [[Gary Speed]] on Scholes and Keane both produced injuries, but while Hamann was able to continue with a [[dead leg]] he sustained in the challenge (and picked up a yellow card for a late tackle on Phil Neville a couple of minutes later),<ref name="harris_296"/><ref name="lawton">{{cite news |first=Matt |last=Lawton |title=Hamann silence over Gullit row |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/3005174/Hamann-silence-over-Gullit-row.html |work=The Telegraph |publisher=Telegraph Media Group |date=11 May 2001 |access-date=12 June 2020 }}</ref> Keane had hurt his left ankle and had to be substituted.<ref name="harris_296"/> Since the usual central midfield stand-in, Nicky Butt, was being rested for the Champions League final, Alex Ferguson chose to bring on forward [[Teddy Sheringham]]; he would play in attack alongside Cole, while Beckham would move into central midfield and [[Ole Gunnar Solskjær]] was moved from up front to the right wing.<ref>{{cite news |first=Colin |last=Mallam |title=FA Cup Final: Sheringham and Scholes make victory look easy |url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/htmlContent.jhtml?html=/archive/1999/05/23/sfgman23.html |work=The Telegraph |publisher=Telegraph Group |date=23 May 1999 |access-date=12 June 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080528005152/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/htmlContent.jhtml?html=%2Farchive%2F1999%2F05%2F23%2Fsfgman23.html |archive-date=28 May 2008 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="harris_297">Harris (2019), p. 297.</ref>

Ninety-six seconds later, Manchester United took the lead. From a free kick just inside their own half, Beckham passed to Scholes, who gave it back to David May; May passed forwards to Andy Cole, who turned away from Dabizas and Hamann before aiming a pass towards Giggs on the left wing, only to find Sheringham in the ball's path. Sheringham played the ball delicately past Lee, who had come in for a tackle, then through the legs of Speed to Scholes, who feinted to shoot, only to pass the ball back to Sheringham, who had continued his run into the penalty area, and his shot went through Harper's legs into the net for the opening goal.<ref name="harris_297"/><ref>Bartram et al. (2013), p. 274.</ref> Further chances fell to Solskjær, Cole and Sheringham in the first half, but all were unable to convert their efforts. Newcastle's best chance of the half fell to Hamann, a powerfully hit long-range shot just being diverted away from goal by Schmeichel.<ref name="double_joy">{{cite news |title=Double joy for Man United |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/fa_cup/350192.stm |website=BBC News |date=22 May 1999 |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref>

====Second half====
[[File:1999 FA Cup Final Scholes goal celeb (cropped).jpg|thumb|Manchester United's players celebrate [[Paul Scholes]]' goal early in the second half to put them 2–0 up.]]
By half-time, Hamann's injury had left him barely able to walk and he told Gullit he could not continue in the match; the two got into an argument in which Gullit accused Hamann of lacking commitment before deciding to bring on Duncan Ferguson in Hamann's place.<ref name="lawton"/> Eight minutes into the half, Manchester United scored a second goal. A long ball from Solskjær was chased by Ryan Giggs, but he was beaten to it by Dabizas, who attempted to clear. Solskjær beat [[Didier Domi]] to Dabizas' clearance and passed to Sheringham on the edge of the penalty area. With his back to goal, Sheringham rolled the ball into the path of Scholes, who shot underneath Harper from 20 yards. Late in the game, Newcastle's [[Silvio Marić]] was one-on-one with Schmeichel but screwed his close-range shot wide of the Dane's right-hand post, and the game finished 2–0 to Manchester United.<ref name="double_joy"/>

