==Post-match==
[[File:1999 FA Cup Final trophy presentation (cropped).jpg|thumb|Despite being substituted early in the game, [[Roy Keane]] received the FA Cup for Manchester United.]]
Although Peter Schmeichel had taken over as captain after Roy Keane was substituted during the game, Keane led the Manchester United team up to the Royal Box to receive the FA Cup from the Prince of Wales (now [[Charles III]]).<ref>{{cite news |title=Prince Charles to present the FA Cup |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-60398067.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160325065408/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-60398067.html |url-status=usurped |archive-date=25 March 2016 |work=The Mirror |publisher=Trinity Mirror |via=Highbeam Research |url-access=subscription |date=26 February 1999 |location=London, England |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref><!-- Also present in the Royal Box were [[Prince Edward, Duke of Kent]], in his role as President of [[The Football Association]], and [[Katharine, Duchess of Kent]]. THEY'RE VISIBLE IN FOOTAGE, BUT NO RELIABLE PRINT SOURCE CONFIRMS IT -->

After the match, Alex Ferguson praised Teddy Sheringham for his immediate impact after being brought on to replace Keane, saying: "Teddy was magnificent. He has proved a point today. He was the key to victory."<ref>Bartram et al. (2013), p. 275.</ref> He also complimented David Beckham on his performance in central midfield after Keane's withdrawal: "Beckham took over Keane's role and was absolutely magnificent... People say that going to Barcelona without Roy Keane will be a major hurdle, but I don't think so now."<ref>Bartram et al. (2013), p. 276.</ref> Ryan Giggs recognised the importance of scoring the first goal early in the game, saying: "It helped getting the early goal then the second. It was hard for Newcastle to get back into the game with the weather like that. After the quick goal we could relax and hit Newcastle on the break."<ref name="delight"/> Meanwhile, Newcastle manager Ruud Gullit was pleased with the effort his players had put in, but bemoaned their mistakes that allowed Manchester United an easy victory: "We were punished for our mistakes. The effort was right. The players worked hard and I can't ask for more, but every time we were on top we made a mistake and that cost us two goals. In the end we had to be thankful they did not get three, four or five."<ref name="delight"/>

Some of Newcastle's fans were less magnanimous in defeat than their manager, as police in [[Newcastle upon Tyne|Newcastle]] reported more than 50 arrests related to violent conduct in the city centre following the match. A crowd of about 500 people had to be dispersed through the use of batons, dogs and horses. Similar incidents had been reported after Newcastle's cup final defeat the year before, but Inspector Jerry Barker of [[Northumbria Police]] said the situation had been brought under control quicker this time.<ref>{{cite news |title=Newcastle arrests follow FA Cup match |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/350414.stm |website=BBC News |date=22 May 1999 |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref>

Having claimed an unprecedented third Double in six seasons,<ref>{{cite news |title=Treble beckons for the Reds |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/350365.stm |website=BBC News |date=23 May 1999 |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref> Manchester United went on to beat Bayern Munich in the Champions League final in Barcelona four days later to claim the Treble, a feat never before achieved by an English club.<ref>{{cite news |title=United crowned kings of Europe |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/353842.stm |website=BBC News |date=26 May 1999 |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref> Their FA Cup triumph would also have qualified them for the [[1999 FA Charity Shield]] and the [[1999–2000 UEFA Cup]], which had absorbed the recently-defunct [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup]] from the following season onwards, but their position as league winners meant that they had already qualified for the Charity Shield and the higher-tier [[1999–2000 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]]; therefore, the other place in the Charity Shield went to league runners-up Arsenal,<ref>{{cite news |first=Dan |last=Warren |title=Beware winning Community Shield |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/4741347.stm |website=BBC Sport |date=5 August 2005 |access-date=9 June 2020 }}</ref> while the UEFA Cup place usually reserved for the cup winners went to Newcastle as cup runners-up.<ref>{{cite web |first=Bert |last=Kassies |title=UEFA European Cups 1999/2000 Results and Qualification |url=https://kassiesa.net/uefa/history/format99.html |website=kassiesa.net |access-date=6 June 2020 }}</ref>

Manchester United decided not to defend their FA Cup title the following season, having instead been invited to compete in the [[2000 FIFA Club World Championship|inaugural FIFA Club World Championship]] in Brazil. The Football Association (FA) encouraged the club to take part in the new tournament as they believed it would help their bid to host the [[2006 FIFA World Cup]]. Alex Ferguson initially suggested that The FA could allow his team a bye to a later round of the competition to help alleviate the fixture congestion that would arise from entering both competitions,<ref>{{cite news |title=Sir Alex looking for FA Cup bye |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/379014.stm |website=BBC News |date=26 June 1999 |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref> but the FA suggested that Manchester United simply pull out of the FA Cup altogether, putting the club in what chairman Martin Edwards called a "no-win" situation. Edwards said: "We're going to get criticised whatever we do. If we don't go people will say that we are selfish and only looking after ourselves and not prepared to help the 2006 World Cup bid. But we will also be criticised if we say we're not going to compete in the FA Cup."<ref name="no_win">{{cite news |title=Edwards derides 'no win' situation |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/379410.stm |website=BBC News |date=28 June 1999 |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref> The offer was also criticised by Manchester United fans; Andy Walsh of the [[Independent Manchester United Supporters' Association]] said, "They've shown very little offer of help in the past when United have looked to ease their fixture congestion. This blinding conversion is all to do with the 2006 campaign."<ref name="no_win"/>

The club ultimately accepted The FA's offer on 30 June,<ref>{{cite news |title=United pull out of FA Cup |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/fa_carling_premiership/381662.stm |website=BBC News |date=30 June 1999 |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref> but they were given until the date of the draw for the second round on 28 October to finalise that decision.<ref>{{cite news |title=Man Utd confirm exit from FA Cup |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/492166.stm |website=BBC News |date=28 October 1999 |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref> Their place in the third round was given to a "[[lucky loser]]", determined by drawing one team out of the 20 losers from the second round;<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup to have 'wild card' entry |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/510168.stm |website=BBC News |date=8 November 1999 |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref> that team was [[Darlington F.C.|Darlington]] of the [[Football League Third Division|Third Division]], who were drawn away to [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Lucky Darlington land Villa trip |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/fa_cup/544737.stm |website=BBC News |date=1 December 1999 |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref> Manchester United were knocked out of the Club World Championship in the group stage after losing to hosts [[CR Vasco da Gama|Vasco da Gama]] and drawing with Mexican club [[Club Necaxa|Necaxa]], their only victory coming against Australian representatives [[South Melbourne FC|South Melbourne]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Fortune fails to save Man Utd |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/special_report/1999/12/99/world_club_championship/599462.stm |website=BBC News |date=11 January 2000 |access-date=9 June 2020 }}</ref> It later turned out that their FA Cup sacrifice was in vain, as [[FIFA]] suggested that their participation in the competition would have no bearing on the vote to host the 2006 World Cup,<ref>{{cite news |title=United sacrifice 'in vain' |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/383027.stm |website=BBC News |date=1 July 1999 |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref> which was ultimately won by the [[German Football Association]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Germany win World Cup vote |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/in_depth/2000/2006_world_cup_decision/822126.stm |website=BBC Sport |date=6 July 2000 |access-date=27 March 2014 }}</ref>

Of the 20 finals played between 1996 and 2015, this final was ranked 19th by ''The Daily Telegraph'' for failing to live up to the promise of the game's early action.<ref>{{cite news |first=Luke |last=Brown |title= FA Cup finals - ranked and rated since 1996 |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2016/05/20/fa-cup-finals---ranked-and-rated-since-1996/manchester-united-2-0-newcastle/ |work=The Telegraph |publisher=Telegraph Media Group |date=20 May 2016 |access-date=12 June 2020 }}</ref>

